# Slice 335 (Codex's answer to the 334 questions, item 3; the book :426): one result requirement is one value of the
# contract whether written by position or by head -- for a formal taken as written (name, return) the value of a part
# is its written field, not the block that holds it. p1, p2 and p3 all require `@: c.FILE`; a copy of each (merge with
# one more field) shows the same part return: a reference to that written type form. The executors run only int
# results, so none of the three is called here. p2 was written with `:=` until M6 4a (slice 345), which gives no part
# any more and is refused by P0 since 350 (Codex CODEX-OPUS-345-4A-REPLY-20261110T041147Z-01 item 2); it is given by head as p3 is.
fn: p1 (int: a) @: c.FILE
    return: 0
end: p1
fn: name: p2; args: (int: a); return: (@: c.FILE); body: (return: 0)
fn: name: p3; args: (int: a); return: (@: c.FILE); body: (return: 0)
R1: merge(p1; (int: zz 1))
R2: merge(p2; (int: zz 2))
R3: merge(p3; (int: zz 3))
